      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the number of different starting lineups for the Grunters' basketball team, given that Ace and Zeppo must always be included and the lineup must consist of 3 more players from the remaining 10 players on the team.\n\n1. **Identify the fixed players:**\n   Ace and Zeppo are already part of the lineup. So, we only need to choose 3 more players from the remaining 10 players.\n\n2. **Calculate the number of ways to choose the remaining 3 players:**\n   The number of ways to choose 3 players from a group of 10 is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{k} \\), which represents the number of ways to choose \\( k \\) elements from a set of \\( n \\) elements without regard to the order of selection. Here, \\( n = 10 \\) and \\( k = 3 \\).\n\n   The combination formula is:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{n}{k} = \\frac{n!}{k!(n-k)!}\n   \\]\n\n   Plugging in the values, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{10}{3} = \\frac{10!}{3!(10-3)!} = \\frac{10!}{3! \\cdot 7!}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify the factorials:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10!}{3! \\cdot 7!} = \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8 \\times 7!}{3! \\times 7!}\n   \\]\n\n   The \\( 7! \\) terms cancel out:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{3!}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate \\( 3! \\):**\n   \\[\n   3! = 3 \\times 2 \\times 1 = 6\n   \\]\n\n5. **Perform the division:**\n   \\[\n   \\frac{10 \\times 9 \\times 8}{6} = \\frac{720}{6} = 120\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of different starting lineups possible is \\(\\boxed{120}\\).",
